From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 23:07:23 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: brcmfmac: pcie: Tell version 6+ firmware what the host
 supports

From shared version 6 on, the firmware reads a host capability word at
offset 84 of the shared area: the IPC version the host speaks and what it
can do.  The vendor host driver always writes it before it sets up the
rings (dhd_set_host_cap()), and so does the Infineon fork of this driver.
brcmfmac never did, so the firmware saw a host of version 0 that can do
nothing.

With the BCM4375 firmware of the Galaxy S10+ (18.41.117, shared flags
0x30040007) that goes wrong in a way that is hard to see: control
messages, events, scans and association all work, but the firmware never
takes a single buffer from the receive post ring (read pointer stuck at 0
with 511 buffers posted), so no data frame reaches the host.  The first
casualty is EAPOL: the access point gives up on the WPA2 handshake with
reason 15, and wpa_supplicant cannot even tell a wrong key from a dead
link.

Write the word the way the vendor driver does for this firmware: the
shared version, no out of band device wake line, and the host ready
doorbell when the firmware advertises it.  That is 0x00001407 here.

Measured on a Galaxy S10+: before the change, 0 packets received on wlan0
through twenty connection attempts; with it, the receive completion ring
moves, the handshake completes, and the phone associates at 270 Mbit/s on
5 GHz with no loss on a ping to the internet, by itself at boot.  The
word alone is enough: ringing the doorbell once more after host ready,
tried along the way, made no difference and is not part of this.

Signed-off-by: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
---
 .../broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c        | 24 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c
index bf6a77a5fb1..c3c8ef0a72b 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c
@@ -237,6 +237,10 @@ static const struct brcmf_firmware_mapping brcmf_pcie_fwnames[] = {
 #define BRCMF_SHARED_DMA_SCRATCH_ADDR_OFFSET	56
 #define BRCMF_SHARED_DMA_RINGUPD_LEN_OFFSET	64
 #define BRCMF_SHARED_DMA_RINGUPD_ADDR_OFFSET	68
+#define BRCMF_SHARED_HOST_CAP_OFFSET		84
+
+#define BRCMF_HOSTCAP_H2D_ENABLE_HOSTRDY	0x00000400
+#define BRCMF_HOSTCAP_DS_NO_OOB_DW		0x00001000
 
 #define BRCMF_RING_H2D_RING_COUNT_OFFSET	0
 #define BRCMF_RING_D2H_RING_COUNT_OFFSET	1
@@ -1666,6 +1670,26 @@ brcmf_pcie_init_share_ram_info(struct brcmf_pciedev_info *devinfo,
 	addr = sharedram_addr + BRCMF_SHARED_RING_INFO_ADDR_OFFSET;
 	shared->ring_info_addr = brcmf_pcie_read_tcm32(devinfo, addr);
 
+	/*
+	 * From version 6 on, the firmware takes the host API version and
+	 * what the host can do from this word, and the vendor host driver
+	 * always fills it in before the rings are set up.  Left at zero, the
+	 * BCM4375 firmware of the Galaxy S10+ answers control messages but
+	 * never takes a single buffer from the receive post ring, so no data
+	 * frame, not even EAPOL, ever reaches the host.  The host has no out
+	 * of band device wake line, and takes the host ready doorbell when
+	 * the firmware asks for it.
+	 */
+	if (shared->version >= 6) {
+		u32 host_cap = shared->version | BRCMF_HOSTCAP_DS_NO_OOB_DW;
+
+		if (shared->flags & BRCMF_PCIE_SHARED_HOSTRDY_DB1)
+			host_cap |= BRCMF_HOSTCAP_H2D_ENABLE_HOSTRDY;
+		brcmf_pcie_write_tcm32(devinfo,
+				       sharedram_addr + BRCMF_SHARED_HOST_CAP_OFFSET,
+				       host_cap);
+	}
+
 	brcmf_dbg(PCIE, "max rx buf post %d, rx dataoffset %d\n",
 		  shared->max_rxbufpost, shared->rx_dataoffset);
 
